        "Vaijapur's climate makes April and May the hardest months for study: May is the hottest, with mean daily maxima near 40 degrees Celsius, just as school examinations, the IB May session and Cambridge's May-June series arrive. Lessons then work best early in the morning or after sunset, in shorter blocks, with full timed papers saved for the cooler parts of the day.",
        "The monsoon, from June, is modest in a rain-shadow region but can still disrupt travel and power for a day at a time, and the Godavari and Shivna rise in a heavy year. Ganeshotsav in August or September brings visiting relatives and late evenings, and Navratri and Dussehra follow in October. Plans made at the start of each term, with festival dates marked, avoid last-minute cancellations.",
        "Diwali brings the longest school break of the year in Maharashtra, which is a chance to catch up on Internal Assessment drafts and past papers if planned early and protected from complete rest. December is the coolest month, with lows near 10 degrees Celsius, and is one of the best months for concentrated study before mocks.",
        "Exam windows sit on top. IB Diploma papers fall mainly in May, with results in early July and a November retake open; Cambridge IGCSE sits in May-June and October-November; Pearson Edexcel in January and May-June. Gudi Padwa, Ramzan Eid, Ambedkar Jayanti and Mahashivratri, including the crowds at the Vaijanath Mahadev temple, are sensible dates to agree around in advance.",
